<h1>selection sort</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="sort.html"><em>sort</em></a> algorithm that repeatedly searches remaining items to find the least one and moves it to its final location.  The run time is <a href="theta.html"><em>&Theta;(n&sup2;)</em></a>, where n is the number of elements.  The number of swaps is  <a href="bigOnotation.html"><em>O(n)</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="inplacesort.html"><em>in-place sort</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="bingosort.html"><em>bingo sort</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="strandSort.html"><em>strand sort</em></a>, <a href="heapSort.html"><em>heapsort</em></a>, <a href="fisherYatesShuffle.html"><em>Fisher-Yates shuffle</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Sorting can be done <a href="inplacesort.html"><em>in place</em></a> by swapping the least remaining item with the item in the next position to be filled.  However, this implementation of the algorithm is not <a href="stable.html"><em>stable</em></a>. </p> 
<p> If the (first) least remaining item is inserted, that is, all intervening items moved down (instead of swapping), this algorithm is stable. However, if the items are in an array, rather than, say a <a href="linkedList.html"><em>linked list</em></a>, the number of moves is <a href="bigOnotation.html"><em>O(n&sup2;)</em></a>. The algorithm is then like <a href="bubblesort.html"><em>bubble sort</em></a> with a more complicated control structure. </p> 
<p> <a href="heapSort.html"><em>Heapsort</em></a> can be seen as a variant of selection sort in which  sorted items are arranged (in a <a href="heap.html"><em>heap</em></a>) to quickly find the next item.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
Thomas Baudel's <a href="http://thomas.baudel.name/Visualisation/VisuTri/selectionsort.html" target="_blank">links to animation (Java)</a>. Simple Programming Tutorials' <a href="https://www.algolist.net/Algorithms/Sorting/Selection_sort" target="_blank">explanation (Java and C++)</a>. Flower Brackets <a href="https://www.flowerbrackets.com/selection-sort-java/" target="_blank">explanation (Java)</a>.  <a href="https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python-program-for-selection-sort/" target="_blank">(Python)</a>.
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
Selection sort illustrated through a <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ns4TPTC8whw" target="_blank">Gypsy folk dance</a>. Created at Sapientia University. <br /> <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Ns4TPTC8whw" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen></iframe>
</p>

